The reported fault does not exist in the live lab. A direct ping from PC0 (20.1.1.10) to PC12 (120.1.1.10) completes with 5/5 packets and zero loss, and a traceroute confirms a clean four-hop path: PC0 → r1 → r11 → r12 → PC12. Every physical interface on every router in this path is operationally UP. Every OSPF adjacency across the entire backbone (r1-r3, r1-r11, r3-r2/r4/r9, r4-r5/r6, r5-r6/r8, r6-r7, r2-r10) is in Full state with no stale or missing neighbors. The r12 router runs EIGRP rather than OSPF by design; r11 serves as the OSPF-to-EIGRP redistribution boundary, advertising r12's networks (including 120.0.0.0/8) back into the OSPF domain, and r12 holds a kernel default route via r11 for return traffic. This architecture is intact and functional. There is no dead interior backbone link, no missing OSPF adjacency, and no black-holed prefix anywhere on the PC0-to-PC12 path. The user's premise — that a core link went down during the maintenance window — is incorrect. The network is fully healthy, and the helpdesk ticket should be closed as a false alarm with no corrective action required.
